= i
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The sharp bound form of the <Erdős-Ko-Rado theorem> is as follows. If $1\leq r\leq n/2$ and $\mathcal F\subseteq\binom{[n]}r$ is an <intersecting family>, then
$$
\boxed{|\mathcal F|\leq\binom{n-1}{r-1}.}
$$
The <uniform set family> of all $r$-element <subsets> containing one fixed point attains the bound. We prove it by the <Katona circle method>.

First establish the <cyclic interval intersection bound>. In any fixed cyclic ordering of $[n]$, at most $r$ cyclic intervals of length $r$ can form an <intersecting family>. If there is a selected interval, rotate the ordering so that it ends at position $n$ and consists of positions $n-r+1,\ldots,n$. Intervals ending at positions $r,\ldots,n-r$ are disjoint from it and cannot be selected. Of the remaining intervals, one ends at $n$; the others have endpoints paired as
$$
(j,\ j+n-r),\qquad 1\leq j\leq r-1.
$$
The two intervals in each pair are disjoint: one wraps around position $n$ and the other lies in the intervening gap, since $n\geq2r$. At most one of each pair belongs to the <intersecting family>, giving at most $1+(r-1)=r$ intervals.

Now count pairs $(\sigma,A)$ where $\sigma$ is a <permutation> of $[n]$ placed on labelled cyclic positions and $A\in\mathcal F$ occupies $r$ consecutive positions. Each <permutation> contributes at most $r$ pairs by the <cyclic interval intersection bound>. Each fixed $A$ contributes $n\,r!\,(n-r)!$ pairs: choose its starting position and order the elements inside and outside its interval. Since $r<n$, the starting position is unique for each such placement. Consequently
$$
|\mathcal F|\,n\,r!\,(n-r)!\leq r\,n!,\qquad |\mathcal F|\leq\frac{r}{n}\binom nr=\binom{n-1}{r-1}.
$$
This completes the <Erdős-Ko-Rado theorem> proof. At $n=2r$, complementary $r$-element <subsets> are the only disjoint pairs, so choosing one member of every complementary pair gives an extremiser. This describes all equality cases at that boundary. The range $n\geq2r$ is essential: for $n<2r$, the entire <uniform set family> is an <intersecting family>.

= ii
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

A <bounded-size transversal kernel> gives a <finite intersection witness for cross-intersecting families>, even when the two <set families> are infinite. We first prove the kernel statement: for any <set family> $\mathcal F$ whose members have size at most $r$, and any integer $s\geq0$, there is a finite <subset> $\mathcal F_0\subseteq\mathcal F$ with
$$
|\mathcal F_0|\leq M(r,s):=\sum_{j=0}^{s}r^j
$$
such that every <set> $H$ of size at most $s$ is a <hitting set> for $\mathcal F_0$ if and only if it is a <hitting set> for $\mathcal F$.

Use <mathematical induction> on $s$. If $\mathcal F$ is empty, take $\mathcal F_0=\varnothing$. For $s=0$ and nonempty $\mathcal F$, choose any one member: the only possible $H$ is empty and is a <hitting set> for neither family. For the induction step, choose $A_0\in\mathcal F$. For each $x\in A_0$ apply the inductive statement to
$$
\mathcal F_x=\{A\in\mathcal F:x\notin A\}
$$
with parameter $s-1$, obtaining $\mathcal F_{x,0}$. Put $\mathcal F_0=\{A_0\}\cup\bigcup_{x\in A_0}\mathcal F_{x,0}$. Its size is at most $1+rM(r,s-1)=M(r,s)$. If $H$ is a <hitting set> for $\mathcal F_0$, choose $x\in H\cap A_0$. Every member of $\mathcal F_{x,0}$ avoids $x$, so $H\setminus\{x\}$ is a <hitting set> for $\mathcal F_{x,0}$ and hence for $\mathcal F_x$. Members of $\mathcal F$ containing $x$ already meet $H$. Thus $H$ is a <hitting set> for all of $\mathcal F$. The reverse implication follows from inclusion. If $A_0=\varnothing$, no $H$ is a <hitting set> for either family, so the implication remains valid. This finite branching proof never assumes $\mathcal F$ is finite.

Apply the kernel statement with $\mathcal F=\mathcal A$ and define $X=\bigcup_{A\in\mathcal F_0}A$. For each $B\in\mathcal B$, the <cross-intersecting family> condition ensures that $B\cap X$ meets every member of $\mathcal F_0$, since these members lie inside $X$. As $|B\cap X|\leq s$, it therefore meets every $A\in\mathcal A$. We obtain
$$
\boxed{A\cap B\cap X\ne\varnothing\quad\text{for every }A\in\mathcal A,\ B\in\mathcal B,\qquad |X|\leq r\sum_{j=0}^{s}r^j.}
$$
In particular, a positive-integer-valued choice valid whether or not $0\in\mathbb N$ is
$$
\boxed{f(r,s)=1+r\sum_{j=0}^{s}r^j.}
$$
Here the constant term of the sum is $1$, including when $r=0$. If either <set family> is empty, $X=\varnothing$ suffices. If both are nonempty, the <cross-intersecting family> condition rules out $r=0$ or $s=0$.

